 1. `handle(proto)` reads the body and the model (`requestBody`, `requestModel`), then calls `serveAgent`. Every route that reads an agent's body (each API, count_tokens, Gemini's, embeddings, images, videos, System One, the Codex backend path) reads it through `readBoundedRequestBody` in [`request_bounds.go`](../../internal/gateway/request_bounds.go). It decodes a `Content-Encoding` of `gzip` or `zstd` (`decodedBody`; Codex sends zstd to a custom provider's `/v1/responses` too, #1223) and drops the header, so the provider is sent plain JSON, with one exception: a ChatGPT account's turn (POST `CodexBase`/responses, from the accounts loop in `forwardOnce` or relayed by `codexUpstreamOn`) is compressed again with zstd and a window of at most 2 MiB, as Codex's own client sends it (`provider.CodexCompressed`; openai/codex `responses_request_compression` and `http-client/src/request.rs`). It is compressed once the body is final, and again on each try. `/responses/compact`, an API key's requests and every other provider stay plain. Sent plain, a 1.1 MB text-only turn was answered 503 (ylorn on X). The plain body is what magpie keeps and shows, and `codexDropped` judges a reset by the bytes that went on the wire. `codexDropped` also reads Envoy's 507 "exceeded request buffer limit while retrying upstream" (the backend failed and the front couldn't retry a body that large) as that reset, at any size, told as a 503 so magpie, Codex and pi ask again and nobody rests (#2034). The size limit (`defaultBodyLimit`, 512 MB) holds for the decoded bytes (413), and any other encoding is refused 415. It was 128 MB until a long Codex session that had read many images went past it decoded, its inline screenshots resent each turn, and got the 413 on all five of Codex's retries (#1422, `TestImageHeavyCodexTurnIsRead`). magpie's own 413 says it is magpie's gateway limit, with the size, and that the provider was not asked (`overLimit`); `writeError` adds to any 413 it isn't holding that this is a byte limit, not the model's context, and to `/compact` or start a new conversation (`TestOverLimitSaysWhoseAndWhatToDo`). Every shape but Gemini's types a 413 `request_too_large`, Anthropic's own type, which pi's overflow table knows: pi compacts and tries once more rather than stopping on an OpenAI-shaped `api_error` (@wilsonxuuuu on X, `TestPayloadTooLargeSaysRequestTooLargeToPi`). `keyLimited` reads a limited key's compressed body the same way, so its reservation is the decoded size. `serveAgent` runs the [middleware](gateway-middleware.md) chain, then `serve`.
 2. `serve` redacts secrets in the request (`redacted`; `redact.MaskJSON` leaves ids alone, and in any text a backend's id (a UUID, or 32 hex digits or more), or an element token cua-driver hands the model for computer use (`s0000002a:14`, #3782), goes as written: no rule, model label or value known from before masks a piece of one (a phone number at the end of `…-a13800138000`, a card number across a UUID's groups), and a whole one is masked only by the user's own words and rules or as a credential, by the password, API key and token rules or a secret rule whose field is named for one (`api_key=`, `refresh_token`), never one named for an id, a cursor, a request or a digest (`secret_id`, `next_page_token`, `Idempotency-Key`, `secret_hash`), nor cua-driver's own `element_token` (any other name with `element` in it, `element_api_key`, is a credential's as before), a bare `key`, a serial number, or what the model takes a UUID or an element token for, nor an element token known from before (one the model labelled in prose a turn earlier) (`noIDs`, [`internal/redact/ids.go`](../../internal/redact/ids.go)); and a tool's name and its MCP server's (`namespace`, `server_label`, `server_name`, #3143); the request's tool definitions and output schemas, found by their place in each protocol (top-level `tools`, `tool_choice`, `response_format`, `text`, `generationConfig`, `stream_options`, the client's ids in `client_metadata` and the like, and the tools of a Responses `additional_tools` or `tool_search_output` item), go as written save their descriptions, so a word the user masks can't break a grammar or an enum (#1911); and what the vendor sealed with a signature, a thinking block, Gemini's thoughts or Responses' encrypted reasoning, goes back as the vendor wrote it: only the values masked elsewhere in the request, and secrets masked before, are put back as their placeholders; a value masked once, at least 8 characters long, is masked again wherever a request has it, as an exact match whatever words are around it, so what the vendor wrote about a placeholder doesn't go back with the value in it, `redact/known.go`; a text masked for a vendor before, such as a system prompt or a turn already sent, is masked as it was then, so a value a rule first finds later in a conversation doesn't change the prompt the vendor has cached and rewrite all of it to the cache, `sentKnownSpans`), resolves the model to a provider or a group, and refuses what can't be served. The reply has the values put back on its way to the agent (`redact.Writer`, a stream event by event, a whole body at its end): a placeholder the model wrote as it was given, or as models rewrite one (spaces inside the braces, a brace or both dropped, another case), is the value again when what is left names one magpie made, and one split across events is held until it is whole (`redact.Restore`, `partialTail`, [`internal/redact/redact.go`](../../internal/redact/redact.go)). A request whose provider and fallbacks, or whose group's models, all skip redaction (`unredactedRoute`, `provider.SkipsRedaction`: set *Send requests unmasked*, `Unredacted`, with every address on this machine or the local network) goes to them as the agent wrote it; the log and recent calls keep the masked body. count_tokens and embeddings follow the same rule. What a routing group's Jev is told of the turn (the message, the user's message before it and the reply to it) is masked all the same unless Jev's own provider skips redaction too (`maskedState` in `askJev`, [`decide.go`](../../internal/gateway/decide.go)): a remote Jev is none of the group's members. The masking is the built-in plugin `redact`'s (`gwmw.Masker`, [Plugin runtime](plugin-runtime.md)); when the settings ask for masking and no masker serves, or it fails, the request is a 503 and nothing is sent (`redacted`, `gwmw.Masking`).
