@@ -112,7 +112,7 @@
       <tr><th>Switch</th><th>What it finds</th></tr>
       <tr><td><span class="ui">Mask secrets</span></td><td>Private key blocks (<code>-----BEGIN … PRIVATE KEY-----</code>). API keys by their shape: <code>sk-…</code> (OpenAI, Anthropic, OpenRouter), <code>ghp_</code>/<code>github_pat_</code>, <code>glpat-</code>, <code>AIza…</code>, <code>xox…-</code>, <code>sk_live_</code>, <code>AKIA</code>/<code>ASIA</code>, <code>hf_</code>, <code>gsk_</code>, <code>xai-</code>, <code>npm_</code>, <code>pypi-</code>, <code>dop_v1_</code>, <code>SG.</code>, and relay keys like <code>oc_sk_…</code>. JWTs. The password in <code>user:password@host</code>. The value of a field named for a secret, as <code>.env</code> files and configs have them: <code>DB_PASSWORD=…</code>, <code>api_key: …</code>, <code>client_secret</code>, <code>token</code>, <code>credential</code>. Your own <span class="ui">Masking rules</span>.</td></tr>
       <tr><td><span class="ui">Mask personal data</span></td><td>Emails, mainland China mobile numbers, Chinese ID numbers and bank card numbers whose checksum adds up. Also on by default: US Social Security numbers, passport numbers (after <em>passport</em>, 护照 or パスポート), IBANs, birthdays (next to <em>birthday</em>, <em>DOB</em> or 生日, and over a year ago, so today&rsquo;s date stays), MAC addresses and serial numbers (after <em>S/N</em>). Off until you turn them on, since agents send them in nearly every request: the user name in home folders, user and computer names (<code>user@host</code>), public IP addresses and cloud storage buckets.</td></tr>
-      <tr><td><span class="ui">Masked words</span></td><td>Each word you listed, exactly, as <code>{{TERM_…}}</code>.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><span class="ui">Masked words</span></td><td>Each word you listed, exactly, as <code>{{TERM_…}}</code>. A date, such as <em>1990-05-12</em>, matches that day however it is written: <em>1990/5/12</em>, <em>1990.05.12</em>, <em>1990年5月12日</em>, <em>19900512</em>, <em>May 12, 1990</em>, and <em>13/05/1990</em> style only where the day can’t be read as a month. Never another date or a version like <em>1990.5.12.1</em>.</td></tr>
       <tr><td><span class="ui">Find with a local model</span></td><td>Under <span class="ui">How it’s found</span>. A model on this computer finds what no rule can: a name or an address in free text. It needs <span class="ui">Mask secrets</span> or <span class="ui">Mask personal data</span> on, and finds under the same switches. The menu beside it picks the model: AstrLink Guard (Chinese and English, a 39 MB download, few false alarms in code, on a Mac it runs on the Neural Engine; the default) or OpenAI’s Privacy Filter (1.6 GB, uses the GPU, masks more ordinary values in code). <span class="ui">Confidence threshold</span> under it is how sure the model must be before a value is masked: Privacy Filter’s default is 0.5, AstrLink Guard’s 0.8, its publisher’s. Higher masks fewer ordinary values, and lets more real names and addresses through: the rules after the model drop most false alarms already, so the default is where we measured the fewest real values lost.</td></tr>
     </table></div>
     <p>A placeholder is the kind plus eight characters of a keyed hash. The same value gets the same placeholder every time, so the conversation reads the same turn after turn and the vendor’s prompt cache stays valid. The values are held in memory only.</p>
